# Mobile Styling Rules

## Primary Rule: Always Use Colors from shared/constants/theme.css

**NEVER use hardcoded colors, hex values, or direct CSS color values in components.** Always use the colors defined in `shared/constants/theme.css` via NativeWind utility classes or theme parsing utilities.

## Usage Patterns

### Using Theme Colors in className

✅ **CORRECT:**

```tsx
import { parseThemeColorFromClassName } from '@shared/constants/theme';
import { useColorScheme } from '@/hooks/use-color-scheme';
import tw from 'twrnc';
import { cn } from '@/lib/utils';

// In your component
const colorScheme = useColorScheme();
const { processedClassName } = parseThemeColorFromClassName(className, colorScheme);
const finalClassName = cn(processedClassName);
const styles = tw`${finalClassName}`;

// Use theme colors in className strings
<View className="bg-background text-foreground">
  <Text className="text-foreground-secondary">Secondary text</Text>
  <View className="bg-brand text-foreground">Brand button</View>
  <View className="border-destructive bg-destructive/10 text-destructive">
    Error message
  </View>
  <View className="text-success bg-success/20 border-success">
    Success message
  </View>
  <View className="text-warning bg-warning/20">Warning message</View>
</View>
```

### Using Theme Colors Directly with Hooks

✅ **CORRECT:**

```tsx
import { useThemeColor } from '@/hooks/use-theme-color';

// Get theme color directly
const backgroundColor = useThemeColor({}, 'background');
const brandColor = useThemeColor({}, 'brand');
const textColor = useThemeColor({}, 'foreground');

// Use in styles
<View style={{ backgroundColor, color: textColor }}>
```

### Using NativeWind with Theme Colors

✅ **CORRECT:**

```tsx
import tw from 'twrnc';
import { cn } from '@/lib/utils';
import { parseThemeColorFromClassName } from '@shared/constants/theme';
import { useColorScheme } from '@/hooks/use-color-scheme';

const colorScheme = useColorScheme();
const { processedClassName } = parseThemeColorFromClassName('bg-brand text-foreground', colorScheme);
const styles = tw`${cn(processedClassName)}`;
```

❌ **INCORRECT:**

```tsx
// Don't use hardcoded colors
<View className="bg-white text-black">
<View style={{ backgroundColor: '#000000' }}>
<View className="bg-[#000000]">

// Don't use colors that aren't defined in shared/constants/theme.css
// Check theme.css to see what colors are available
```

## Opacity/Transparency

You can use opacity modifiers with theme colors in className strings:

```tsx
// Opacity is parsed and applied automatically
<View className="bg-brand/20 border-brand/60 text-foreground/80">
<View className="bg-success/10 text-success/90">
<View className="border-destructive/50 bg-destructive/5">
```

The `parseThemeColorFromClassName` utility automatically handles opacity values (e.g., `/60` = 60% opacity).

## Font Sizes

### SwiftUI Text Styles

**NEVER use Tailwind's generic text size classes like `text-xs`, `text-sm`, `text-base`, `text-lg`, `text-xl`, etc.** Always use ONLY the SwiftUI text style font sizes defined in `mobile/global.css`.

Use SwiftUI text style font sizes defined in `mobile/global.css`:

✅ **CORRECT:**

```tsx
import { parseFontSizeFromClassName } from '@shared/constants/theme';

// Parse font size from className
const { fontSize, processedClassName } = parseFontSizeFromClassName('font-body text-foreground');
// fontSize = 17, processedClassName = 'text-foreground'

// Available font styles:
// font-large-title (34px)
// font-title (28px)
// font-title2 (22px)
// font-title3 (20px)
// font-headline (17px) - automatically applies font-semibold
// font-body (17px)
// font-callout (16px)
// font-subheadline (15px)
// font-footnote (13px)
// font-caption (12px)
// font-caption2 (11px)

<Text className="font-headline text-foreground">Headline Text</Text>
<Text className="font-body text-foreground-secondary">Body Text</Text>
```

❌ **INCORRECT:**

```tsx
// NEVER use Tailwind generic text size classes
<Text className="text-sm text-foreground">Small text</Text>
<Text className="text-lg text-foreground">Large text</Text>
<Text className="text-base text-foreground">Base text</Text>
<Text className="text-xl text-foreground">XL text</Text>
<Text className="text-xs text-foreground">XS text</Text>

// Instead, use SwiftUI text styles
<Text className="font-caption text-foreground">Caption text</Text>
<Text className="font-body text-foreground">Body text</Text>
<Text className="font-headline text-foreground">Headline text</Text>
```

The `Text` component automatically:
- Parses font sizes from `font-{style}` or `text-{style}` classes
- Applies `font-semibold` for headlines (unless overridden by explicit font-weight class)
- Applies default text color if none specified
- Converts font size classes to pixel values

### Font Weights

Font-weight classes are **separate** from font-size and are valid to use:

✅ **CORRECT:**

```tsx
<Text className="font-title2 font-bold text-foreground">Bold Title</Text>
<Text className="font-large-title font-extrabold text-foreground">Extrabold Large Title</Text>
<Text className="font-body font-semibold text-foreground">Semibold Body</Text>
```

Available font-weight classes: `font-thin`, `font-extralight`, `font-light`, `font-normal`, `font-medium`, `font-semibold`, `font-bold`, `font-extrabold`, `font-black`

### Font Families

Font-family classes like `font-mono` can be combined with font size classes:

✅ **CORRECT:**

```tsx
<Text className="font-mono font-caption text-foreground-secondary">CODE_VARIABLE</Text>
<Text className="font-mono font-footnote text-brand font-bold">code_value</Text>
```

The `font-mono` class applies monospace font-family and works alongside SwiftUI text style font sizes.

## Component Styling Patterns

### View Components

```tsx
import { View, ThemedView } from '@/components/view';
import tw from 'twrnc';
import { cn } from '@/lib/utils';
import { parseThemeColorFromClassName } from '@shared/constants/theme';
import { useColorScheme } from '@/hooks/use-color-scheme';

// ThemedView automatically applies background color and SafeAreaView
<ThemedView className="flex-1">
  <View className="p-4 bg-background-secondary rounded-lg">
    Content
  </View>
</ThemedView>

// Regular View with theme color parsing
const colorScheme = useColorScheme();
const { processedClassName } = parseThemeColorFromClassName(className, colorScheme);
const styles = tw`${cn(processedClassName)}`;
<View style={styles}>
```

### Text Component

```tsx
import { Text } from '@/components/native/text';

// Text component automatically handles:
// - Font size parsing (font-body, font-headline, etc.)
// - Theme color parsing (text-foreground, text-brand, etc.)
// - Default text color application
// - Headline font-weight (font-semibold by default, can be overridden)

<Text className="font-headline text-foreground">Headline</Text>
<Text className="font-body text-foreground-secondary">Body text</Text>
<Text className="font-caption text-placeholder">Caption</Text>
<Text className="font-title2 font-bold text-foreground">Bold Title</Text>
<Text className="font-mono font-footnote text-brand font-bold">Monospace code</Text>
```

**Nested Text components** are supported for inline styling variations:

```tsx
<Text className="font-large-title text-foreground">
  Build Faster with <Text className="text-brand">{BOILERPLATE_NAME}</Text>
</Text>
```

### Icon Component

```tsx
import { IconSymbol } from '@/components/native/icon';

// Icon component automatically handles:
// - Size parsing from className (size-*, w-*, h-*)
// - Theme color parsing (text-brand, text-foreground, etc.)
// - Platform-specific icon rendering (SF Symbols on iOS, Material on Android, Tabler on Web)

<IconSymbol name="house" className="size-6 text-brand" />
<IconSymbol name="house.fill" className="w-8 h-8 text-foreground/60" />
```

### GlassView Component

```tsx
import { GlassView } from '@/components/glass-view';

// GlassView automatically:
// - Parses theme colors from className
// - Uses native liquid glass when available
// - Falls back to BlurView with theme colors

<GlassView className="bg-foreground-secondary/10 border-foreground-secondary/20">
  Content
</GlassView>
```

## NativeWind Usage

### Basic Pattern

```tsx
import tw from 'twrnc';
import { cn } from '@/lib/utils';

// Always parse theme colors first, then apply other classes
const colorScheme = useColorScheme();
const { processedClassName } = parseThemeColorFromClassName(className, colorScheme);
const finalClassName = cn(processedClassName, otherClasses);
const styles = tw`${finalClassName}`;
```

### ScrollView contentContainerStyle

For ScrollView's `contentContainerStyle`, you can use `tw` template literal directly:

✅ **CORRECT:**

```tsx
import { ScrollView } from 'react-native';
import tw from 'twrnc';

<ScrollView contentContainerStyle={tw`p-6 gap-8`}>
  {/* content */}
</ScrollView>
```

This pattern is useful for layout spacing that doesn't involve theme colors.

### Combining Theme Colors with Other Classes

```tsx
// Theme colors are parsed and converted to arbitrary values
// Other Tailwind classes work normally
const className = 'bg-brand text-foreground p-4 rounded-lg shadow-sm';
const { processedClassName } = parseThemeColorFromClassName(className, colorScheme);
// processedClassName = 'bg-[#hex] text-[#hex] p-4 rounded-lg shadow-sm'
```

## Theme Awareness

All colors in `shared/constants/theme.css` are theme-aware and automatically support light/dark mode. The CSS variables are defined for both `:root` (light mode) and `:root.dark` (dark mode).

Use `useColorScheme()` hook to get the current color scheme:

```tsx
import { useColorScheme } from '@/hooks/use-color-scheme';

const colorScheme = useColorScheme(); // 'light' | 'dark'
```

## Available Theme Colors

Check `shared/constants/theme/theme.css` for the complete list. Common colors include:

- `background` - Main background color
- `background-secondary` - Secondary background color
- `foreground` - Main text/foreground color
- `foreground-secondary` - Secondary text color
- `placeholder` - Placeholder text color
- `brand` - Brand/accent color
- `destructive` - Error/destructive actions
- `success` - Success states
- `warning` - Warning states

## When to Use This Rule

- When adding new mobile components or styling existing ones
- When choosing colors for text, backgrounds, borders, or any visual elements
- When working with NativeWind classes that involve colors
- When parsing className strings for theme colors
- When setting font sizes using SwiftUI text styles
- When using theme-aware hooks like `useThemeColor`

## Notes

- Colors are defined in `shared/constants/theme.css` as CSS variables
- Theme colors in className strings must be parsed using `parseThemeColorFromClassName` before applying with NativeWind
- Font sizes use SwiftUI text style conventions (defined in `mobile/global.css`)
- **NEVER use Tailwind's generic text size classes (`text-xs`, `text-sm`, `text-base`, `text-lg`, etc.). ONLY use SwiftUI text style font sizes (`font-caption`, `font-body`, `font-headline`, etc.)**
- Font-weight classes (`font-bold`, `font-extrabold`, etc.) are **separate** from font-size and are valid to use alongside SwiftUI text style font sizes
- Font-family classes like `font-mono` can be combined with font size classes
- The `Text` component automatically handles font size and color parsing
- The `IconSymbol` component automatically handles size and color parsing
- Spacing utilities (`mt-*`, `mb-*`, `gap-*`, `p-*`, etc.) are standard Tailwind utilities and are valid to use
- Always use `useColorScheme()` to get the current theme mode for color parsing
- Use `useThemeColor()` hook for direct color access without className parsing
- Only use colors that exist in `theme.css`
- If you need a new color, add it to `theme.css` first (both `:root` and `:root.dark` selectors), then add it to the `@theme` block, then use it via NativeWind classes
